## Decision and scope

This record supersedes [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
and the merge-on-subject rule in [triage](/kb/triage/dedupe).
The accepted historical record is preserved. The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471)
is also preserved, but its “one issue, one fix” narrative does not match the data its components render.

This is an analysis of the supplied support-kb export through 4 October 2026,
not proof that either product defect has been fixed. The desk supports multiple products;
this incident concerns sched-prod booking dates and calendar invites.

<Callout kind="warn">
Read the computed views before relying on prose, a recent review date, or a “known” status.
Different subjects can describe the same cause, and identical subjects can hide different causes.
</Callout>

## What the export actually shows

<IncidentSummary />

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

### Parser: month-first parsing of day-first dates

The shared parser reads dates month first even for tenants whose locale writes the day first.
For example, R-41200 (harbour.dental, app) describes 7 April becoming 4 July.
Use the tenant directory and channel to place the report, not the browser locale or intake tag.
The majority cause belongs to #4471; keep it separate from the form-hint defect below.
Engineering should make the shared parsing contract agree with the tenant date format and verify
ambiguous day/month inputs through app, email, and the older booking form.

### Hint: booking-v3 gives day-first tenants a month-first hint

The booking-v3 date field prints MM/DD/YYYY, while the form interprets input in the tenant's
local date order. This is a separate defect for form reports from day-first tenants using booking-v3.
R-41230 (copper.studio, form) and R-41251 (pale.harbour, form) belong here even though intake tagged them parser.
Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour use this form in the export.
Correct the hint and its input contract; a shared-parser fix alone does not resolve this mismatch.

### Evidence limits

The computed footnote identifies reports describing a one-day move. The desk's provided classifier
places them in the parser group by tenant and channel. Preserve that count, but investigate their
original scheduling/time-zone evidence before claiming a separate time-zone defect or a confirmed mechanism.
These are reported symptoms, not reproduced product failures. Do not invent a third cause from wording alone.

## Arrival is not the same as merge time

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The export starts receiving reports in April, but merges into #4471 begin on 2 July.
The July merge spike includes earlier reports; it does not prove a July spike in new failures.
Use receivedAt for arrival trends and mergedAt for desk activity. The desk continues working,
so refresh the export and check the repository's latest issues and comments before handoff.

## Replacement triage rule

1. Count each report ID once; export pages overlap. Filter to the incident being investigated.
2. Read the body, tenant configuration and channel. The browser's locale and intake tag are clues, not authority.
3. Use causeOf and sameCause from src/lib/kb/causes.ts with tenantOf from src/lib/kb/locales.ts.
   For a day-first tenant, booking-v3 form reports are hint; other channels/forms are parser.
4. Merge only when the cause is established and matches. Unknown tenants or unplaced reports stay separate for investigation.
5. Record report IDs and the count on each engineering issue; link both issues from the incident.
6. Review the operational triage page against this decision. Keep decision 0001 unchanged as history.

<DedupeExplorer />

The examples include app versus email for the same cause, identical subjects with different causes,
different subjects with the same cause, and an unknown tenant that must not be merged automatically.
Counts, examples, timeline and the signature below are produced by the supplied code at render time.

